{"qid":"Q117","names":["Ghana"],"title":"Ghana","endonym":{"native":"Ghana","lang":"ak"},"capsule":"The Republic of Ghana became the first sub-Saharan African country to achieve independence from British colonial rule on 6 March 1957, under Kwame Nkrumah, who declared a republic in 1960. Nkrumah pursued pan-African socialism and ambitious industrialization — most notably the Akosombo Dam on the Volta — before being overthrown in a military coup in 1966. Ghana experienced repeated civil-military oscillations through the 1970s and 1980s under Jerry Rawlings, before a democratic constitution in 1992 established a stable multiparty system that has governed since, making Ghana one of West Africa's most consistent democracies.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Ghana's modern international boundaries are well established through decolonization treaties; there are no significant active border disputes affecting its recognized territorial extent.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Ghana","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghana"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kwame Nkrumah","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kwame_Nkrumah"}],"britannica":"place/Ghana","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Ghana","rulers":[{"qid":"Q8620","name":"Kwame Nkrumah","start":1960,"end":1966,"image":"1989 CPA 6101.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/1989%20CPA%206101.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":"USSR Post","attributionRequired":false}},{"qid":"Q356728","name":"Joseph Arthur Ankrah","start":1966,"end":1969,"image":"Joseph Arthur Ankrah in America (cropped).jpg","imageUrl":